## Introduction
Molecules that bind to DNA or RNA can serve in the regulation of gene expression, maintenance of cellular DNA and DNA repair, as well as controlling expression of the gene product. Thus, molecules that target specific nucleotide sequences are potential therapeutics for genetic diseases and cancers. Scientists have been working on strategies to selectively turn off specific genes in diseased tissues for the past 30 years. In this regard, antisense oligodeoxynucleotides (ODNs) were first tested in the 1980s — both in vitro and in vivo. In the 1990s, ribozymes with both antisense and catalytic properties were successfully introduced to the field. Small interfering RNA (siRNA) is currently the fastest growing agent of this 'antigene' field for target validation and therapeutic applications. Antigene molecules have the potential to further our understanding of the human genome, as well as enabling the development of efficacious gene therapies for human diseases. Despite their promising activities in vitro, however, delivery issues and the cytotoxicity of these therapeutic molecules limits their clinical utility.
