Another emerging technology to alter the expression of specific genes involves the creation of artificially designed DNA-binding proteins that allow any genes of interest to be targeted. These artificial transcription factors comprise a DNA-binding domain, which provides specificity, and an effector domain, which mediates the desired function (e.g. upregulation or downregulation). Judicious selection of the effector domain makes it possible to alter the biological activity of the transcription factor without changing the DNA specificity.
This review considers recent advances in developing nucleotide-targeting molecules. Applications of these molecules and their delivery to target cells, which is crucial to promote therapeutic activity, are also discussed.
## Nucleic-acid-based gene targeting
Since the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved the phosphorothioate antisense nucleotide fomivirsen (VitraveneTM; designed to bind to mRNA and prevent expression of specific genes) for human therapeutic use in 1998, scientists have been searching for more effective nucleotide-based molecules to turn off selected genes or their products. Among the different types of molecule that have been developed to inhibit gene expression in a sequence-specific manner, three major classes have emerged: chemically modified antisense ODNs, ribozymes and siRNAs. The mode of action of these molecules is illustrated in Figure 1.
## Aptamers
Aptamers are small single- or double-stranded oligonucleotides (DNA or RNA) that can bind to a wide range of target molecules in a manner that is conceptually similar to antibody recognition of an antigen. Aptamers that specifically bind to target molecules can be selected from combinatorial nucleic acid libraries of up to \(>10^{15}\) different sequences [1,2]. They offer the advantage of an in vitro selection process (i.e. there is no need to use animals or cell cultures) and have proved successful as aptamers are not toxic or immunogenic. Among the many aptamers that have been reported, the antivascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) aptamer, Macugen (pegaptanib sodium injection), is currently the most successful example. Macugen is designed to treat age-related macular degeneration. This aptamer specifically binds the VEGF165 protein, the isoform of VEGF that induces angiogenesis (the formation
of new blood vessels), and antagonizes the binding of VEGF165 to its receptor.
## Triplex-forming oligonucleotides (TFOs)
Triplex-forming oligonucleotides (TFOs) have been shown to bind to DNA in a site-specific manner and to induce mutations and recombination at chromosomal loci in mammalian cells [3], as well as downregulating gene expression via hydrogen-bonding interactions. High-affinity binding of TFOs is limited to polypurine/polypyrimidine sites in the target DNA. Crucial issues regarding TFO-based technologies include the development of new oligonucleotide analogs with improved binding affinity, better target selectivity, and sufficient stability in the intracellular environment.
## Decoy ODNs
cis-Element double-stranded ODNs, referred to as 'decoy ODNs', are powerful tools for transcriptional repression. Decoy ODNs reduce promoter activity owing to their ability to bind transcription factors that would otherwise bind to a specific sequence (cis-element) in the promoter region of the target gene. The advantage of a decoy is that it is protected against degradation from nucleases by the introduction of phosphorothioate bonds within the DNA backbone. The first clinical trials of an NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB}\) decoy for the prevention of restenosis have been performed, and the results indicate the clinical usefulness and safety of this therapeutic [4]. However, there are still unresolved problems with effective decoy delivery systems.
Recently, a new class of nucleotide analog — termed locked nucleic acids (LNAs) — was used to design decoy
Recently, a new class of nucleotide analog — termed locked nucleic acids (LNAs) — was used to design decoy molecules for NF- \(\mathbf{\nabla\cdotKB}\) [5]. LNAs contain a methylene linkage between the \(2^{\prime}\) oxygen and the \(4^{\prime}\) carbon of the ribose ring, offering the LNA-modified ODNs higher stability in serum, increased solubility in water, and partial resistance against DNase I endonuclease.
## Ribozymes and DNAzymes
Ribozymes are catalytic RNAs that bind and cleave specific target mRNAs, thus inhibiting protein synthesis. Different classes of ribozymes, including hammerhead and hairpin ribozymes, have been discovered in natural systems or obtained by in vitro selection. Phase I and Phase II clinical trials with ribozymes designed for the treatment of viral infections and cancer have been conducted.
DNAzymes are analogs of ribozymes in which the RNA backbone is replaced by DNA to provide improved stability. DNAzymes offer significant advantages over ribozymes, including easier synthesis and increased chemical and biological stabilities. The intra-tumoral injection of a DNAzyme that targets the VEGFR2 transcript and acts as a tumor angiogenesis inhibitor has shown recent promise [6].
## siRNA
Since RNA interference (RNAi) was first discovered as a naturally occurring antiviral defense mechanism in plants, extensive research efforts have led to the development of this phenomenon as a potential therapeutic strategy. RNAi is mediated by siRNAs, which recognize homologous mRNA sequences and initiate mRNA destruction through an activated RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC) [7]. siRNA was initially thought to recognize
the target sequence with high specificity; however, recent reports have shown that target recognition is far more degenerate \([8^{\bullet},9^{\bullet}]\). Rigorous research will be required to improve the design algorithms for siRNAs.
RNAi can be induced through the introduction of synthetic siRNA or by the vector-driven expression of short hairpin RNAs (shRNAs). A major hurdle, however, is the abundance of nucleases that degrade unprotected RNAs within seconds of systemic administration (discussed below). Nevertheless, RNAi-based therapeutics for the treatment of macular degeneration and respiratory syncytial virus infection have entered Phase I clinical trials.
## Artificial zinc-finger proteins
Zinc-finger proteins can bind to DNA in a sequence-specific manner and are widespread in eukaryotic cells. The \(\mathrm{Cys}_{2}\mathrm{His}_{2}\) motif is characteristic of one class of zinc-finger proteins (Figure 2), and most natural zinc-finger proteins contain multiple copies of this structural unit. Here, we focus on various platforms that have been developed for the application of artificial zinc-finger proteins (AZPs). The reader is referred elsewhere [10,11] for details on designing zinc-finger motifs. The sequence specificity of AZPs was demonstrated by microarray analysis [12].
## Transcriptional regulation
Many diseases have been shown to result from the overexpression or underexpression of specific genes. Thus, transcription factors (TFs) and other regulators of gene expression have become increasingly attractive targets for therapeutic intervention.
Artificial zinc finger proteins (AZPs). AZPs can be fused to many different effector domains (EDs) for different applications. (a) AZP (green) fused to a transcription activation domain such as VP16. The basal transcription complex, including RNA polymerase II (Pol II), is recruited via an ED such as VP16 so that transcription of the target gene is induced. This activator ATF can be easily converted to a repressor ATF by switching the effector domain to a repressor domain such as KRAB (Kru¨ ppel-associated box). (b) AZP fused to a nuclease domain of the FokI restriction endonuclease (FN). Like the original FokI restriction endonuclease, ZFNs require dimerization of the nuclease domain to produce an efficient double-stranded break. (c) AZP (green) fused to a catalytic domain of the M.Ssrl methyltransferase. Promoter methylation represses transcription through the recruitment of methylation-dependent transcriptional repressors.
Artificial transcription factors (ATFs) typically comprise a DNA-binding domain (DBD) and an effector domain (ED). The DBD is made from zinc fingers and is responsible for the recognition of a specific DNA sequence, whereas the ED mediates the desired function (Figure 3a). ATF's can be used as either transcriptional activators or repressors, depending on the ED. In the absence of an ED, the ATF acts as a weak transcription repressor; this might result from a cryptic repressor domain or from steric interference [13].
To downregulate endogenous genes at the transcriptional level, a repressor ATF would be expected to have higher efficiency than RNAi, because only two copies of DNA have to be targeted per cell as compared with multiple mRNA molecules in RNAi approaches. However, moderate levels of gene suppression might be sufficient for many practical applications. Synergistic silencing of a
target gene can be achieved using a combination of ATF technology and RNAi [14]. The efficacy of ATFs introduced through viral-vector-based gene therapy have been demonstrated in rodents [15,16,17] and rabbits [18].
## Genome modification
AZPs have also been used for site-directed genome modification [19,20]. Artificially designed zinc finger nucleases (ZFNs) have been utilized to cleave and stimulate mutations at specific genomic sites in plant [21] and mammalian cells [22] with high efficiency.
ZFNs consist of an AZP fused to the cleavage domain of the FokI restriction endonuclease (Figure 3b). These chimeric endonucleases can be used to induce double-stranded breaks (DSBs) at a specific chromosomal locus in a sequence-specific manner, thereby promoting site-specific homologous recombination in a variety of organisms. To achieve effective DSBs, the cleavage domains of each ZFN monomer must come together to form a dimer [23]; thus, binding of two four-finger ZFN monomers to a target site is required to induce local homologous recombination. In addition, four-finger ZFNs were found to be less cytotoxic than three-finger ZFNs, owing to their higher sequence specificity [22].
ZFNs have been developed for the treatment of X-linked severe combined immune deficiency (SCID) and sickle-cell anemia (SCA). The correction ratios reported so far are \(10–15\%\) for SCID in somatic K562 cells, and \(3\%\) in the \(\upbeta\) globin gene for SCA. Other AZP-based site-specific nucleases have been developed [24,25,26]; however, the cleavage activities of these DNA cutters in vivo have not yet been reported.
Further applications of AZP technology such as chromatin modification, including DNA methylation [27,28] and DNA integration, have been reviewed elsewhere [29] (Figure 3c).
## The delivery of therapeutics
The gene-targeting molecules introduced above and therapeutic genes all need to be delivered into target cells to yield their therapeutic activities. The simplest method for the delivery of therapeutic molecules is the direct introduction of the molecules into target cells. This approach is limited in its application, however, because it can only be used with certain tissues. Moreover, large amounts of molecules are required, owing to extremely low delivery efficiencies. Current technologies to overcome such problems are discussed below.
## Lipid-based delivery systems
One of the first nonviral delivery systems to be developed involved the creation of liposomes, artificial lipid spheres with an aqueous core that can retain water-soluble molecules. Liposomes carrying therapeutic agents are capable
of passing through the membrane of the target cell to deliver their cargo.
A large number of cationic lipids have been widely reported for the synthesis of liposomes used for the delivery of different molecules. Recent progress includes the development of tissue-specific gene delivery using immunoliposomes, in which functionalized antibodies were fused to lipid bilayers [30,31]. Despite significant advantages over viral delivery methods, such as nonimmunogenicity, the cytotoxicity of cationic lipids is of great concern.
## Polymer-based delivery systems
Cationic polymers have been widely used in gene delivery. These materials combine with anionic DNA to form a DNA–polymer complex that can interact with negatively charged cell surfaces through the cationic portion of the complex. Among polymers explored as gene carriers, polyethyleneimine (PEI) has received the most attention, owing to its ability to bind strongly to negatively charged DNA molecules. Biodegradable polymers such as poly(L-lysine) (PLL) are known for their lower toxicity
and higher biocompatibility than PEI; however, the amino acid backbone of PLL is somewhat immunogenic. To overcome this drawback, a derivative of PLL, poly[α-(4-aminobutyl)-L-glycolic acid], was synthesised in which the ester bond was substituted with amide. This polymer exhibited higher transfection efficiency and lower immunogenicity and cytotoxicity than the original PLL polymer [32].
Chitosan is a naturally occurring biodegradable polymer with a strong affinity for DNA. Owing to its mucoadhesive properties, chitosan has been successfully used for the oral and nasal delivery of therapeutic genes [33,34].
## Cell-penetrating peptides
Biological membranes are generally impermeable to large hydrophilic molecules such as proteins, limiting the development of peptide drugs and therapeutic proteins. However, a series of small protein domains, termed cell-penetrating peptides (CPPs) or protein transduction domains (PTDs), have been shown to cross biological membranes (reviewed in [35]) and could facilitate delivery to mammalian cells. Although the mechanism of CPP-mediated transduction across the membrane is not fully understood, novel classes of CPPs continue to emerge and could contribute to cell-targeted and in vivo delivery of drugs.
CPPs have been successfully employed for the delivery of ATFs [13]. In one such study, a regulatory protein was designed to incorporate a CPP fused to an ATF comprising a nuclear localization factor (NLS), an artificial zinc finger protein and an effector domain (Figure 4). The NLS mediates import into the nucleus, where the zinc finger protein then recognizes its binding site on the promoter of the target gene. The basal transcriptional complex, including RNA polymerase II, is recruited and transcription of the target gene is induced (Figure 5).
A major disadvantage of this promising technology is the lack of targeting specificity; for therapeutic purposes, specificity is required to selectively target diseased cells. To overcome this problem, in vivo screening of phage-displayed peptide libraries has been used to identify peptides that could allow phage to be delivered specifically to the cell surface of the desired tissue, such as blood vessels (reviewed in [36]). These peptides are called 'homing peptides' and comprise a relatively short stretch of peptides (less than 40 amino acids in length). Some of the isolated peptides not only exhibit homing properties, but also have cell-penetration characteristics [37]. When conjugated to peptides, or even to small molecules, these peptides have proved successful for delivery [38]. For example, CPPs specific for tumor cells, which penetrate
into tumor endothelial cells but not into normal tissues or cells, could be useful for directing therapeutic agents to tumors \([39^{\bullet}]\).
Peptide-based technologies that employ CPPs could also be a promising solution for the delivery of oligonucleotide therapeutics [40] (reviewed in [41]).
## Conclusions
Nucleic acid-based molecules that hybridize with specific nucleotide sequences are potential therapeutics for genetic diseases and cancers. Among the many different types of molecule developed, some have already made it to market and many have been in clinical trials. Despite their promising activities in vitro, however, delivery and cytotoxicity issues have limited their clinical utility.
As an alternative to the use of nucleotides, an approach based on the use of ATFs has been successfully used to manipulate gene expression [13,15]. Zinc-finger nucleases — ATFs in which the effector domain takes the form of a nuclease — have also been designed and can be used for genome engineering, including the correction of mutations involved in monogenic inherited diseases; however, it will be necessary to further investigate whether zinc-finger nucleases can fulfill the very strict requirements for therapeutic applications. Although many researchers have extensively investigated sequence-specific DNA-binding domains, it is clear that more structural information (including crystallographic data) will help further refine the design of ATFs [42].
All of the molecules discussed here must be delivered efficiently into the target cells to yield therapeutic activities, and we have considered several vectors employed for this purpose. The most recent advances in this area include the use of antibody fragments fused to protamine [43], engineered nonpathogenic Escherichia coli [44], and bacterial ghosts [45]. Other influential papers have described the use of protamine–antibody fusion proteins to deliver siRNAs to HIV-infected or envelope-transfected cells [46] or the use of 117-nucleotide bacteriophage \(\phi29\) -encoded packaging RNA fused to siRNAs [47]. These methods might not only be applicable to siRNAs, but could be used to deliver other classes of molecule.
In summary, several technologies are currently available for researchers to 'target the human genome'. Each offers advantages and disadvantages, yet the rapid advances in this area are likely to promote future success in the clinic. Novel methodologies for the delivery of gene-targeting therapeutics are also under development and will complement the approaches described.
